#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101959 :tutorial/content "When multiplying two matrices in general, we apply the same approach as usual:\n\n- First, we verify that the two matrices are conformable for multiplication.\n- Then, we multiply each row in the first matrix by each column in the second.\n\nFor example, let's find $AB$, where\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$</div>\n</div>\n\nFirst, we verify the conformability for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}} \\times \\color{#2E8B57}{\\fbox{3}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{#2E8B57}\\checkmark}{\\cdot} \\underset{{\\color{#2E8B57}\\fbox{3}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}}}{B} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}}}{C}\n$$\n\nSo the matrices are conformable for multiplication, and the product is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let's set up the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\\n\t1 & -1 & 3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\n\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        -5 & 2  \\\\\n\t2 & -2  \\\\\n\t-4 & 1 \n    \\end{bmatrix}}_{B} \\\\ &=\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\ast & \\ast  \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\t}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the entries of the product matrix (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $c_{11}$, the entry at the intersection of the first row and first column, we calculate the dot product of the first row of $A$ and the first column of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{#4169E1}-2 & \\color{#4169E1}2 & \\color{#4169E1}1 \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}1 & \\color{gray}-1 & \\color{gray}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n         \\color{red}-5 & \\color{gray}2  \\\\\n\t\\color{red}2 & \\color{gray}-2  \\\\\n\t\\color{red}-4 & \\color{gray}1\n    \\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}10}} & \\ast  \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}10}} = ({\\color{#4169E1}{-2}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + {\\color{#4169E1}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}})\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{12}$, we calculate the dot product of the first row of $A$ and the second column of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{#4169E1}-2 & \\color{#4169E1}2 & \\color{#4169E1}1 \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}1 & \\color{gray}-1 & \\color{gray}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{gray}-5 & \\color{red}2  \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}2 & \\color{red}-2  \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}-4 & \\color{red}1\n    \\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-7}}  \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-7}} = ({\\color{#4169E1}{-2}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{#4169E1}{2}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{21}$, we calculate the dot product of the second row of $A$ and the first column of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{gray}-2 & \\color{gray}2 & \\color{gray}1 \\\\\n\t\\color{#4169E1}1 & \\color{#4169E1}-1 & \\color{#4169E1}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n         \\color{red}-5 & \\color{gray}2  \\\\\n\t\\color{red}2 & \\color{gray}-2  \\\\\n\t\\color{red}-4 & \\color{gray}1\n    \\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & -7  \\\\\n        \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-19}} & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-19}} = {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\\color{#4169E1}{-1}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{#4169E1}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}})\n$$\n\nFinally, to find $c_{22}$, we calculate the dot product of the second row of $A$ and the second column of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{gray}-2 & \\color{gray}2 & \\color{gray}1 \\\\\n\t\\color{#4169E1}1 & \\color{#4169E1}-1 & \\color{#4169E1}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{gray}-5 & \\color{red}2  \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}2 & \\color{red}-2  \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}-4 & \\color{red}1\n    \\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & -7  \\\\\n        -19 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}7}}\n    \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}7}} = {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + ({\\color{#4169E1}{-1}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{#4169E1}{3}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nAB = \n\t\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 \\\\ -19 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ce402944-0109-5d9d-93d5-394323fbc469" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2993 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
